01:12:00,000 --> 01:12:06,000
Hugo is a 2011 American adventure drama film directed and produced by
Martin Scorsese, and adapted for the screen by John Logan.

13
01:18:00,000 --> 01:18:06,000
Based on Brian Selznick's 2007 book The Invention of Hugo Cabret, it tells the story of a boy who lives alone in the Gare Montparnasse railway station in
Paris in the 1930s, only to become embroiled in a mystery surrounding his late father's automaton and the pioneering filmmaker Georges Méliès.

14
01:24:00,000 --> 01:24:06,000
Hugo is Scorsese's first film shot in 3D, about which the filmmaker remarked, "I found 3D
to be really interesting, because the actors were more upfront emotionally.

15
01:30:00,000 --> 01:30:06,000
Their slightest move, their slightest intention is picked up much more precisely."
The film was released in the United States on November 23, 2011.

16
01:36:00,000 --> 01:36:06,000
Despite receiving considerable acclaim from critics, Hugo was a financial
disappointment, grossing only $185 million against its estimated $150 million budget.

17
01:42:00,000 --> 01:42:06,000
The film received 11 Academy Award nominations (including Best Picture), more than any other film that year, winning a leading five
awards: Best Cinematography, Best Art Direction, Best Sound Mixing, Best Sound Editing, and Best Visual Effects.

18
01:48:00,000 --> 01:48:06,000
It was also nominated for eight BAFTAs, including Best Director, and winning two, and was
nominated for three Golden Globes, including Scorsese's third win for Best Director.

